{"number": 119, "text": "      Sig       get                                         |\n\n                get + noun = receive/buy/find, etc.\n\n                                                   you get it\n                 you don’t have something os                           you have it\n\n                     ©) | got an email from Sam this morning. (= receive)\n                     ©       your sweater. Where did you get it? (= buy)\n                     ©)     hard to get a job at the moment? (= find)\n                     ©  “ls Liz here?” “Yes. I'll get her for you”\n                get hungry / get cold / get tired, etc. (get + adjective) = become:\n                                             you get hungry\n                                                                    you are hungry\n                        not hungry\n\n                     ©   Ifyou don't eat, you get hungry.\n                     ©)  Drink your coffee. It’s getting cold.\n                     ©   I'm sorry your mother is sick. | hope she gets better soon.\n                     ©)  It was raining very hard. We didn’t have an umbrella, so we got very wet.\n                 also get married                          ©)  Nicole and David are getting married soon.\n                     get dressed (= put your clothes on)       | got up and got dressed quickly.\n                     get lost (= lose your way)            ©)  We  didn’t have a map, so we got lost.\n\n                 get to A place = arrive:\n                     O   | usually get to work before 8:30. (= arrive at work)\n                         We  left Boston at 10:15 and got to Ottawa at 11:45.\n                 get here/there (without to):\n                     ©   How  did you get here? By bus?\n                 get home  (without to):\n                         What  time did you get home last night?\n                                                                                        I\n                 get in/ou